                "event": "Flash Flood Warning",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS San Francisco CA",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued November 18 at 7:05PM PST until November 18 at 8:30PM PST by NWS San Francisco CA",
                "description": "FFWMTR\n\nThe National Weather Service in San Francisco has extended the\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nThe Timber burn area in...\nWestern Monterey County in central California...\n\n* Until 830 PM PST.\n\n* At 705 PM PST, CHP has reported an observed debris flow into NB\nHighway One lanes in the vicinity of Big Sur Lodge and boulders in\nthe roadway at Hurricane Point. A car was reported stuck in the\ndebris flow near Big Sur. Doppler radar and automated rain gauges\nindicated that heavy rain was falling over the Timber Burn Area.\nBetween 3 and 6 inches of rain have fallen. The expected rainfall\nrate is 0.5 to 0.75 inches in 1 hour. Additional rainfall amounts\nof 1 to 3 inches are possible in the warned area. Conditions remain\nfavorable for additional moderate to heavy rainfall due to\norographic lift as the main rainband exits the county. Flash\nflooding is ongoing. Excessive rainfall over the burn scar will\nresult in debris flow moving through the Drainages within Lafler,\nCastro and Graves Canyons, as well as drainages between Grimes and\nCastro Canyons. The debris flow can consist of rock, mud,\nvegetation and other loose materials.\n\nHAZARD...Life threatening flash flooding. Heavy rain producing\nflash flooding in and around the Timber Burn Area.\n\nSOURCE...Law enforcement reported.\n\nIMPACT...Life threatening flash flooding of areas in and around the\nTimber Burn Area.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nportions of CA Highway 1 and the Los Padres National Forecast from\nAndrew Molera State Park to Partington Ridge including the town of\nBig Sur.\n\nAt 5:43 PM PST CHP reported a debris flow across NB Highway 1 lanes\nin the vicinity of Big Sur Lodge. One hour rain rates have crossed\n0.7\" with additional heavy rain expected this evening into tomorrow\nas scattered showers with the potential for moderate to heavy rain\nand thunderstorms. CHP additionally reported boulders in the NB lane\nof Highway One at Hurricane Point at 6:11 PM local time. CHP reported\na car stuck in the debris flow at Big Sur.",
                "instruction": "Move away from recently burned areas. Life-threatening flooding of\ncreeks, and roads is likely. The heavy rains will likely trigger\nrockslides, mudslides, and debris flows in steep terrain. Impacted\nareas include locations along Highway 1 near mile marker 50 south of\nAndrew Molera State Park to mile maker 38. Other areas impacted\ninclude Pfeiffer Big Sur State Park Campground and Partington Ridge,\nstructures along the Big Sur River, Mule Creek Canyon, and adjacent\ndrainages and watershed outlets.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
                "parameters": {
