                "senderName": "NWS San Francisco CA",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued November 18 at 4:58PM PST until November 18 at 7:30PM PST by NWS San Francisco CA",
                "description": "FFWMTR\n\nThe National Weather Service in San Francisco has extended the\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nThe Plaskett burn area in...\nSouth Central Monterey County in central California...\n\n* Until 730 PM PST.\n\n* At 458 PM PST, Doppler radar and automated rain gauges indicated\nheavy rain falling over the Plaskett Burn Area. Between 1.5 and 2.5\ninches of rain have fallen. The expected rainfall rate is 0.5 to\n0.75 inches in 1 hour. Additional rainfall amounts of 1 to 3 inches\nare possible in the warned area. Flash flooding is ongoing or\nexpected to begin shortly. Excessive rainfall over the burn scar\nwill result in debris flow moving through the Alder, Plaskett, and\nPrewitt Creeks. The debris flow can consist of rock, mud,\nvegetation and other loose materials.\n\nHAZARD...Life threatening flash flooding. Heavy rain producing\nflash flooding in and around the Plaskett Burn Area.\n\nSOURCE...Radar and automated gauges.\n\nIMPACT...Life threatening flash flooding of areas in and around the\nPlaskett Burn Area.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nareas near CA Highway 1 mile marker 17 to mile marker 11 including\nLos Burros Road.",
                "instruction": "Move away from recently burned areas. Life-threatening flooding of\ncreeks, and roads is likely. The heavy rains will likely trigger\nrockslides, mudslides, and debris flows in steep terrain. Impacted\nareas include locations along Los Burros Road, areas near Plaskett\nCreek Campground and structures along the Alder, Plaskett, and\nPrewitt Creeks.\n\nBe aware of your surroundings and do not drive on flooded roads.\n\nStay away or be swept away. River banks and culverts can become\nunstable and unsafe.\n\nPlease report observed flooding to local emergency services or law\nenforcement and request they pass this information to the National\nWeather Service when you can do so safely.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
